<DocsLayout title="Builds" activeNav="/platform/builds">
  <h1>Builds</h1>
  <p>
    A build record is the platform's account of one build attempt: which profile, which identifier,
    its status, tags, log, and artifacts. The console is where you read that history and start new
    builds; pipelines and runners write to it.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="builds"
    alt="Build history with filters and four builds in completed, running, failed and pending states"
    caption="Build history for one application, with the filter row above it."
  />

  <h2>Reporting mode decides who owns a build</h2>
  <p>
    The badge next to each row — <strong>MANUAL</strong>, <strong>AUTOMATIC</strong>,
    <strong>EAS</strong> — is the single most useful thing on this screen. It says who is allowed to
    change the build's status, and it explains why some rows offer actions others do not.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Who reports</th><th>What you can do here</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Manual</strong></td>
        <td>Your own runner, through the API or <code>appmctl stream</code></td>
        <td>Force a status, cancel, tag, attach artifacts</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Automatic</strong></td>
        <td>A CI provider the platform dispatched to</td>
        <td>Cancel (asks the provider), tag. Status and logs are the provider's.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>EAS</strong></td>
        <td>Expo</td>
        <td>Tag. The record mirrors the Expo build.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <strong>Force status</strong> appears only on manual builds, and only while they are not
    terminal. It exists for the case where a runner died without reporting — the build would
    otherwise claim to be running forever.
  </p>

  <h2>Filters</h2>
  <p>
    The filter row narrows history by tags, statuses, pipeline, artifact presence, reporting mode,
    and a date or duration range. Two are worth calling out:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Artifacts: Has / Has not</strong> finds builds that finished but produced nothing,
      which usually means a pipeline succeeded while its upload step did not.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reporting: Manual / Automatic</strong> separates what your runners reported from what
      a provider did, which matters when you are chasing an inconsistency between the two.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tags</h2>
  <p>
    Tags are free-form labels with an optional colour, editable inline from the history rows —
    the <code>+</code> under each build. Unlike status, tags can be set on <em>any</em> build
    regardless of reporting mode, which makes them the way to annotate provider-driven builds from
    outside: a release marker, a ticket number, a channel.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tags also drive filtering here and webhook filtering elsewhere, so a consistent tagging habit
    pays off twice.
  </p>

  <h2>Starting a build</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Run build</strong> on the application header offers two paths:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Manual build</strong> — creates a record your runner then reports against. Choose the
      profile and optionally the identifier; the platform hands back a build id.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pipeline build</strong> — dispatches a configured CI/CD pipeline. The platform creates
      the record and the provider fills it in. Pipelines with required inputs prompt for them here.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A manual build with no runner attached to it simply sits at <code>pending</code>: creating the
    record does not start anything. That is the intended shape for CI that reports its own progress —
    see <a href="/guides/cli/builds/manual-builds">Manual builds</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>One build in detail</h2>
  <p>
    Opening a build shows its log, artifacts, context, and timing. The log is streamed while the
    build runs, so the page fills in as a manual build reports. Long logs are archived, and the
    archive stays downloadable after the live log is truncated.
  </p>
  <p>
    Artifacts are listed with their size and metadata; each is downloadable. Artifacts belong to the
    build, so deleting the build removes them — anything that should outlive a build belongs in
    <a href="/platform/files-and-assets">files</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For provider-driven builds this screen also carries <code>dispatch_status</code> and a link to
    the provider's own run page. A build that failed to dispatch never ran at all, which is a
    credentials or configuration problem rather than a build failure — a distinction worth making
    before reading the log for clues that are not there.
  </p>

  <h2>Doing the same from the CLI</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/guides/cli/builds"><code>appmctl builds</code></a> — list, inspect, create, cancel, tag, and <code>wait</code> for an outcome</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/cli/builds/manual-builds"><code>appmctl stream</code></a> — run a command and upload its output as the build log</li>
    <li><a href="/guides/cli/integrations/pipelines"><code>appmctl pipelines run</code></a> — dispatch a pipeline and get the build id back</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
